The condition that the three creases in the triangle do not intersect means that the point P must lie in some region of the triangle. When the three vertices A, B, and C are folded onto P, the creases that form are the perpendicular bisectors of the segments AP, BP, and CP. Clearly these lines intersect at P and are distinct unless P is a vertex of ABC. Equivalently, when these creases do not intersect, P is interior to the triangle formed by the three intersection points of consecutive creases. It is also true that any point P in that triangle creates three distinct creases. It is well-known that that triangle is the so-called medial triangle of ABC.

The medial triangle of a triangle is one-half the original triangle's area (easy to see by noting that the medial triangle's vertices are half the distance from the respective vertices in the original triangle, and are collinear with the original vertices, making that triangle a triangle with a midpoint on each side, which must be one-half the area of the original triangle).

\begin{align*}
AB=36, AC=72, \angle B &= 90^{\circ}.\\
BC &= \sqrt{36^2+72^2}=\sqrt{36^2(1+4)} = 36 \sqrt{5} \\
\text{the area of } \triangle ABC &= \dfrac{1}{2}\cdot 36 \cdot 36 \cdot 5 = 36^2 \cdot \dfrac{5}{2} = 36^2 \cdot \dfrac{\sqrt{5}}{2} \sqrt{5}\\
\text{the area of the medial triangle} &= 18^2 \cdot \dfrac{\sqrt{5}}{2}
\end{align*}

The medial triangle is thus $ \pi r^2-h^2.$ One may find either the radius or the height since either is underdetermined in creating right triangles within the medial triangle. Either of the ratios will yield a solution.

The radius can be found by the distance from the apex C to the perpendicular bisector of segment AP. Thus, the distance is $\dfrac{36 \sqrt{5}}{2} ( \dfrac{1}{3} + \dfrac{1}{2}) = 6 \sqrt{5}.$ Divide this by two to get $3 \sqrt{5}$ and finish the problem. $q + r + s = 2 + 1 + 5 = \boxed{8}.$</think> <answer>$q + r + s = 2 + 1 + 5 = \boxed{8}$.

